6281279 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281279 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281279 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186279770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281279 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "faab536c-ba69-524a-89bb-9d4a9ee45c2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281647 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281647 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281647 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281647 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d1/d1a19303937f1d515ca820516e10a73974b00f83884b05abb58dee49c81207f4.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the upper half plane bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281647 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec31a85b-303d-5111-a311-f0cb89d6a30f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281647 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}=8\\pi .\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{8\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }\\cdot 64\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =4M.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281693 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281693 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281693 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00a2dbbe-8269-5871-944e-208bd7aa03cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281708 #edb/ref 1080 "70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281708 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281708 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01f3ea30-3f9d-5508-ab40-ee2dba4f0888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281819 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281819 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281819 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ec9ea01-bfeb-5dfa-bb0d-e8dda89e8cde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281930 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186281930 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/r